#X declare -lib ceammc;
#X obj 299 50 cnv 1 466 38 empty empty empty 17 7 0 10 -245760 -1 0;
#X text 304 50 synchronization of multiple values without overflowing
stack loops;
#X obj 1 1 cnv 5 765 40 empty empty sync 20 20 0 20 -104026 -4096 0;
#X obj 603 11 ui.link @background_color 0.39216 0.39216 0.39216 @title
[ceammc/sync] @url sync-help.pd;
#X obj 711 8 sync;
#X obj 230 93 cnv 1 535 411 empty empty empty 17 7 0 10 -237178 -1 0;
#X text 240 98 The value sent to one inlet is repeated to all other
outlets \, except the one paired with that inlet. This prevents infinite
loops.;
#X text 240 153 The problem: when two or more GUI objects (sliders
\, number boxes \, knobs) need to show the same value and also modify
each other \, a direct bidirectional connection causes an infinite
loop — A sends to B \, B sends back to A \, A sends again to B \, and
so on \, until the Pd stack overflows. The object [sync] solves this:
it remembers which inlet the value came from and forwards it to all
other outlets except the paired one. This way the chain naturally terminates
and both widgets stay in sync.;
#X text 240 299 Typical use cases:;
#X text 250 324 • Linked sliders — for example \, L and R sliders of
a stereo balance: moving one should update the other without an infinite
loop.;
#X text 250 379 • Knob + number box — a [ui.knob] and a [floatatom]
showing the same parameter and updating each other.;
#X text 250 419 • MIDI controller and GUI — a physical knob on a MIDI
controller and a [ui.knob] in the patch: MIDI input goes through [sync]
to the GUI \, and GUI changes go back through [sync] to MIDI output.;
#X text 50 552 simple example;
#X floatatom 50 581 5 0 0 0 - - -;
#X obj 136 581 sync;
#X floatatom 232 581 5 0 0 0 - - -;
#X text 50 610 more complex;
#X floatatom 50 639 5 0 0 0 - - -;
#X floatatom 126 639 5 0 0 0 - - -;
#X floatatom 203 639 5 0 0 0 - - -;
